    {
      "name": "entry-calls-a-sibling-which-is-a-surface-call-again",
      "_note": "The entry reading applies to the ENTRY FRAME only. An action the entry calls is an ordinary surface call, so its declared inputs evaluate in the caller's scope as they always did — one hop in, the rule reverts.",
      "actions": {
        "handle": {
          "inputs": {
            "order": "order"
          },
          "body": "dsx.action.tag({ id: order.id })"
        },
        "tag": {
          "inputs": {
            "id": "id"
          },
          "body": "dsx.variable.tagged = id"
        }
      },
      "scope": {},
      "runAction": "handle",
      "runPayload": {
        "order": {
          "id": "ord_7"
        }
      },
      "expectStore": {
        "tagged": "ord_7"
      },
      "expectEvents": []
    },
    {
      "name": "a call-site argument WINS over the declared input's caller-scope reading",
      "_why": "`inputs=` has two readings and both are correct: at a bare call the name is an expression evaluated in the CALLER's scope, and at a call that PASSES an object the name is the argument. When the caller has no such name, evaluating the expression yields the absent sentinel and, applied last, ERASES the argument the call site supplied - so `openDoc({ name: 'x' })` from a caller with no `name` in scope bound null. Swift and Kotlin already merged the argument OVER the input; the TS runner did not, which made this the one divergence in the workflow grammar.",
      "actions": {
        "caller": {
          "body": "callee({ word: 'passed' })"
        },
        "callee": {
          "inputs": {
            "word": "word"
          },
          "body": "dsx.variable.seen = word"
        }
      },
      "scope": {},
      "run": "caller",
      "expectStore": {
        "seen": "passed"
      },
      "expectEvents": []
    },
    {
      "name": "an unsupplied declared input still reads the caller's scope",
      "_why": "the other reading, unchanged: a caller that HAS the name passes it implicitly, which is what makes `pick()` inside a list row work without an argument.",
      "actions": {
        "caller": {
          "body": "const word = 'from the caller'; callee()"
        },
        "callee": {
          "inputs": {
            "word": "word"
          },
          "body": "dsx.variable.seen = word"
        }
      },
      "scope": {},
      "run": "caller",
      "expectStore": {
        "seen": "from the caller"
      },
      "expectEvents": []
    },
    {
      "name": "dsx.action spelling binds the passed argument the same way the bare call does",
      "actions": {
        "caller": {
          "body": "dsx.action.callee({ word: 'explicit' })"
        },
        "callee": {
          "inputs": {
            "word": "word"
          },
          "body": "dsx.variable.seen = word"
        }
      },
      "scope": {},
      "run": "caller",
      "expectStore": {
        "seen": "explicit"
      },
      "expectEvents": []
    },
    {
      "name": "await on a bare action call runs it, with its argument",
      "_why": "`await x({ … })` reached the SYNCHRONOUS expression evaluator, which knows nothing about declared actions: the action never ran and the argument vanished, silently. Both native runners dispatch the same call, so this was the one divergence in the workflow grammar - and the documented way to sequence one action after another.",
      "actions": {
        "caller": {
          "body": "await callee({ word: 'awaited' }); dsx.variable.after = 'ran'"
        },
        "callee": {
          "inputs": {
            "word": "word"
          },
          "body": "dsx.variable.seen = word"
        }
      },
      "scope": {},
      "run": "caller",
      "expectStore": {
        "seen": "awaited",
        "after": "ran"
      },
      "expectEvents": []
    },
    {
      "name": "an awaited action's return value is the value of the expression",
      "actions": {
        "caller": {
          "body": "const got = await callee({ word: 'x' }); dsx.variable.seen = got"
        },
        "callee": {
          "inputs": {
            "word": "word"
          },
          "body": "return word + '!'"
        }
      },
      "scope": {},
      "run": "caller",
      "expectStore": {
        "seen": "x!"
      },
      "expectEvents": []
    }
  ]
